[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 475,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 432,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66862,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 432,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myoepithelial neoplasms of the thoracic cavity are very rare tumours that share morphological, immunophenotypic, and genetic features with their counterparts in soft tissue, salivary gland, bone, and skin. The term &ldquo;myoepithelioma&rdquo; is applied to benign myoepithelial tumours, whereas malignant myoepithelial tumours are designated &ldquo;myoepithelial carcinoma&rdquo;.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 476,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 433,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66863,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 433,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8982/0 Myoepithelioma</p>\r\n<p>8982/3 Myoepithelial carcinoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 477,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 434,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66864,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 434,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2C25.Y &amp; XH3CQ8 Other specified malignant neoplasms of bronchus or lung &amp; Myoepithelioma</p>\r\n<p>2C25.Y &amp; XH43E6 Other specified malignant neoplasms of bronchus or lung &amp; Malignant myoepithelioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 478,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 435,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66865,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 435,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> myoepithelial tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 479,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 436,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66866,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 436,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66867,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>About half of pulmonary myoepithelial neoplasms are located in the large airways (endobronchial or endotracheal), with the remaining being intraparenchymal {26645726}. Myoepithelial tumours may also arise in the chest wall, mediastinum, and heart {12960802; 18043035}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66868,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "<p>Imaging shows central endobronchial tumours to be well circumscribed, homogeneous, polypoid, and sessile, whereas intraparenchymal tumours are either well-defined nodules or irregular masses with calcifications. Presenting features include cough with or without haemoptysis and dyspnoea, indicating obstruction in central tumours. Intraparenchymal tumours may be asymptomatic.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-03T09:25:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66869,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Thoracic myoepithelial tumours are very rare, most often occurring in adults (mean age: 52&nbsp;years) {26645726}. Myoepithelial carcinomas predominate in children {18043035}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66870,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is no association with smoking or other carcinogens.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66871,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>EWSR1</em> gene rearrangements are common in myoepithelial tumours, identified in about 50% of cases, with diverse fusion partners, including <em>POU5F1</em>, <em>PBX1</em>, <em>ZNF444</em>, <em>KLF17</em>, <em>PBX3</em>, and <em>ATF1</em> {20815032; 25231231; 26645726}; occasional cases show alternative <em>FUS</em> gene rearrangements {25706482; 26645726}. A subset of myoepithelial carcinomas harbour homozygous deletions of <em>SMARCB1</em> {24585572}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66872,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "<p>Myoepithelial tumours can be well circumscribed or infiltrative, with a yellow-tan cut surface {26645726}. They vary in size from 15 to &gt;&nbsp;130 mm, with a larger size seen in malignant tumours {26645458}. Malignant tumours may show necrosis and/or haemorrhage.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-02T10:54:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66873,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In contrast to mixed tumours, myoepitheliomas and myoepithelial carcinomas are composed exclusively of myoepithelial cells, without ductal or tubular structures. Myoepithelial tumours are multilobulated, with cells that vary from epithelioid or round and clear to plasmacytoid to spindled and that may be arranged in sheets or nests or have a reticular or trabecular pattern, embedded in myxoid, myxochondroid, or hyalinized matrix. Entrapped type&nbsp;II pneumocytes can be hypertrophic and resemble glands {31567188}. Myoepithelial carcinomas often show marked nuclear atypia, significant pleomorphism, increased mitoses, necrosis, and infiltrative growth {26645458}; some paediatric examples contain sheets of round cells {18043035}. The tumour cells express keratins, EMA, and S100 and variably p63, p40, SOX10, SMA, and GFAP.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66874,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 443,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 487,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 444,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66875,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 444,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rearrangements of <em>EWSR1</em> or <em>FUS</em> can be detected.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 490,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 447,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66876,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 447,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Trabecular, reticular, nested, and/or solid growth of variably epithelioid or spindled cells, with a frequent myxoid or hyalinized stroma</li>\r\n<li>Myoepithelial carcinomas show increased nuclear atypia, mitotic activity, and necrosis</li>\r\n<li>Positivity for EMA/keratins and S100, SOX10, or GFAP</li>\r\n<li>Exclusion of metastasis from other sites</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li><em>EWSR1</em> rearrangements may be helpful in selected cases</li>\r\n</ul>",
        "timestamp": "2021-01-04T10:09:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 488,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 445,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66877,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 445,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 489,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 446,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66878,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 63,
        "headingId": 446,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The presence of necrosis and &ge;&nbsp;5&nbsp;mitoses/2&nbsp;mm<sup>2</sup> correlates with worse prognosis {26645726}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:04:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]